# HyperAudio · License Summary

HyperAudio uses a split-license model to clearly separate the code from the audio artifacts.

---

## License Scope

| Scope | License | File |
|---|---|---|
| **EEL2 Code** (`.eel`) | MIT | [LICENSE-MIT](LICENSE-MIT) |
| **Audio Artifacts** (`.irs`, `.vdc`) and Reports | CC BY-SA 4.0 | [LICENSE-CC-BY-SA-4.0](LICENSE-CC-BY-SA-4.0) |

---

## MIT License (Code)

The EEL2 scripts (`.eel`) are free to use, modify, and distribute for any purpose, including commercial applications. You do not need to credit HyperAudio in your final product, though it is appreciated.

## CC BY-SA 4.0 (Audio Artifacts)

The impulse responses (`.irs`), correction profiles (`.vdc`), and technical reports (`.md`) are licensed under Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0. 

This means:
- **Attribution:** You must give appropriate credit to Noushad M.
- **ShareAlike:** If you remix or modify the audio artifacts, you must distribute your contributions under the same license.

> [!NOTE]
> This split licensing ensures the DSP math remains open and accessible, while preventing the custom tuning curves from being rebranded and sold as proprietary presets.

